Fixed and committed as 67c669b3: yoyo now shuts down every connected stdio MCP server when it exits, including servers that ignore a closed stdin. Build, the full cargo test, clippy -D warnings and fmt --check all pass.

What changed
• `src/mcp_prefix.rs`: a new close_live_mcp_servers(MCP_CLOSE_BOUND) empties the list of live servers and closes each one by killing its process group. All servers close at once, and the whole step is capped at 2 seconds. Calling it a second time does nothing. The doc comment explains the 2s for users: a healthy server closes in milliseconds, so the cap only matters for a server stuck in a call. On SIGTERM, the existing 5s flush plus these 2s stays under the 10s docker stop waits before it sends SIGKILL.
• `src/main.rs`: a new exit_after_shutdown(code) helper closes the servers and then exits.
  • It replaces all 13 process::exit calls that can run after servers have connected: 6 in single-prompt (-p) mode, 5 in piped mode, the -p slash-command refusal, and the --continue-strict failure.
  • For that last one, restore_session had to become async.
  • The 3 normal returns (-p, piped, REPL) call the close directly.
• `src/prompt/sigterm.rs`: the SIGTERM/SIGHUP/SIGQUIT exit also closes servers first.
• Left alone on purpose: 2 exits in main that run before servers connect, plus the early exits in cli.rs, dispatch_sub.rs and config_syntax.rs. No server can be running at those points.

Tests
• Two unit tests: the list is emptied once, and a server that never finishes closing costs at most the cap without holding up the others.
• Two tests against the real binary, using a python3 stub server that ignores stdin closing:
  • a normal -p exit, with a fake provider that answers 401 so the run fails at once instead of sitting through retries;
  • a SIGTERM in the middle of a turn, which is the case measured on the ticket.
• The existing test's spawn code moved into one shared helper so all three tests use the same environment cleanup. It still passes.
• Positive control: I made the close helper return immediately. Both real-binary tests failed by name, because the server started by the real connect outlived yoyo. After restoring it, they passed again.
• REPL `/quit` has no automated test because it needs a terminal. I checked it by hand with script(1), and both stub processes were gone after /quit. My first attempt showed them still alive, but that run was using the stale binary from the positive control; after rebuilding, they were gone.

Not covered: a panic in yoyo, or a server still stuck in a call when the 2s run out, still leaves the server running. I filed that as #1100 before pushing, so "Fixes #1099" is accurate. A SIGKILL of yoyo itself can't be handled. I also updated docs/src/troubleshooting/common-issues.md, which had said such servers outlive yoyo, and added a Day-242 note in ARCHITECTURE.md marking the old "close is skipped" sentence as superseded.
